The web app's bundle includes third-party code.

The build compiles its runtime dependencies into the JavaScript and CSS it
ships, among them React, react-dom and scheduler (MIT, Meta Platforms, Inc.
and affiliates), Radix UI (MIT, WorkOS), lucide-react (ISC, Lucide Icons and
Contributors), class-variance-authority (Apache-2.0, Joe Bell), Firebase
(Apache-2.0, Google LLC), three.js, pdf.js (Apache-2.0, Mozilla Foundation),
pdf-lib, fflate, the noble cryptography libraries and the Inter and JetBrains
Mono typefaces (OFL-1.1). Each keeps its own licence. The Guide's "Built with"
page lists every one by name with its installed version, licence and copyright
holder, and a test fails if a production dependency of the web app is missing
from it (web/src/components/guide/credits.test.ts). Their full licence texts
travel with them in node_modules.

No third-party source is vendored into this repository itself. The programs
jojo TALKS TO — MarkItDown, and whichever model server you point it at — are
installed and run by you on your own machine, and none of their code is in
either app bundle. Their notices are reproduced in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md
anyway, because a person running them deserves to see the terms without going
to look.
